    "captions": [
      {
        "t": 253.22,
        "who": "Teacher",
        "text": "Today, population policies."
      },
      {
        "t": 256.15,
        "who": "Globo",
        "text": "Anti-natalist policies reduce births. Pro-natalist policies increase them. Immigration policies fill labour gaps."
      },
      {
        "t": 263.86,
        "who": "Dad",
        "text": "Pro natalist? Pro, like me."
      },
      {
        "t": 266.14,
        "who": "Globo",
        "text": "Singapore did both. Stop at Two in the nineteen seventies, with higher hospital fees for third children. It worked too well. Fertility fell below replacement, about two point one."
      },
      {
        "t": 278.03,
        "who": "Globo",
        "text": "So, nineteen eighty seven, Have Three or More. And from two thousand and one, the Baby Bonus."
      },
      {
        "t": 284.48,
        "who": "Tara",
        "text": "But fertility was about zero point nine seven in twenty twenty three. Cash rarely beats the cost of housing and tuition. So we rely on immigration too."
      },
      {
        "t": 294.61,
        "who": "Globo",
        "text": "France pays family allowances and subsidises crèches. India's Emergency sterilisations, many under pressure, caused a backlash. Kerala cut births with girls' education instead."
      },
      {
        "t": 306.32,
        "who": "Tara",
        "text": "So policies work best with social change, not against it."
      },
      {
        "t": 310.49,
        "who": "Teacher",
        "text": "Now, today's debate. Was China's One-Child Policy a success?"
      },
      {
        "t": 315.22,
        "who": "Globo",
        "text": "This is about real families, so no jokes. From nineteen eighty, one-child families got cash, housing and school priority. Extra children meant fines or job loss."
      },
      {
        "t": 325.98,
        "who": "Globo",
        "text": "Some areas saw forced abortions and sterilisations. Exceptions included rural families with a firstborn girl, and many minorities."
      },
      {
        "t": 334.77,
        "who": "Tara",
        "text": "Effects. About one hundred and eighteen boys born per hundred girls by twenty ten. Naturally, it's about one hundred and five."
      },
      {
        "t": 343.32,
        "who": "Globo",
        "text": "And the four two one problem. One child may support two parents and four grandparents."
      },
      {
        "t": 349.32,
        "who": "Dad",
        "text": "Six? I can barely support myself."
      },
      {
        "t": 351.98,
        "who": "Globo",
        "text": "China aged fast. It allowed two children from twenty sixteen, and three from twenty twenty one. Even so, its population has fallen since twenty twenty two."
      },
      {
        "t": 362.35,
        "who": "Dad",
        "text": "Arguments for. It slowed growth when China was poor, and eased pressure on food and jobs."
      },
      {
        "t": 368.55,
        "who": "Globo",
        "text": "That's actually a fair point."
      },
      {
        "t": 370.95,
        "who": "Tara",
        "text": "Against. Fertility had already fallen from about six to under three in the nineteen seventies, before it began. And Thailand and Kerala cut births without force."
      }
    ]
