    "startSec": 325.5,
    "endSec": 416.25,
    "durationSec": 90.75,
    "src": "/videos/extreme-climates.mp4",
    "poster": "/videos/posters/extreme-climates.jpg",
    "captions": [
      {
        "t": 327.96,
        "who": "Mum",
        "text": "Home. Hot and raining. Why does Singapore never change?"
      },
      {
        "t": 331.95,
        "who": "Globo",
        "text": "A climate graph shows why. Bars are monthly rainfall, in millimetres. The line is temperature. Check which axis is which."
      },
      {
        "t": 340.05,
        "who": "Tara",
        "text": "Singapore's line is almost flat. Highest minus lowest, the annual range, is just two degrees."
      },
      {
        "t": 346.41,
        "who": "Globo",
        "text": "The Sahara station climbs from twelve degrees in January to thirty six in July. A range of twenty four."
      },
      {
        "t": 353.4,
        "who": "Tara",
        "text": "Total rainfall means adding all twelve bars. Singapore, about two thousand two hundred millimetres. The Sahara station, twenty."
      },
      {
        "t": 361.88,
        "who": "Tara",
        "text": "Antarctica. Warmest minus three, coldest minus twenty six. Minus three take away minus twenty six is twenty three."
      },
      {
        "t": 369.55,
        "who": "Globo",
        "text": "Describe it with TEA. Trend, Evidence with figures, and Anomaly."
      },
      {
        "t": 374.1,
        "who": "Mum",
        "text": "TEA! Finally, a geography rule I can follow. Teh tarik for everyone."
      },
      {
        "t": 379.82,
        "who": "Globo",
        "text": "Recap. One. Extreme means at the limits of life."
      },
      {
        "t": 383.37,
        "who": "Globo",
        "text": "Two. Deserts. Sinking air, no clouds."
      },
      {
        "t": 386.23,
        "who": "Globo",
        "text": "Three. Poles are cold because of angle, atmosphere and albedo."
      },
      {
        "t": 390.65,
        "who": "Globo",
        "text": "Four. Mountains. Colder as you climb."
      },
      {
        "t": 393.52,
        "who": "Globo",
        "text": "Five. Graphs. Add the bars, find the range."
      },
      {
        "t": 397.68,
        "who": "Mum",
        "text": "Next trip! An extreme mountain, five minutes away. Bukit Timah Hill."
      },
      {
        "t": 402.48,
        "who": "Tara",
        "text": "It's about a hundred and sixty metres high, Mum. That's one degree cooler."
      },
      {
        "t": 407.65,
        "who": "Dad",
        "text": "One degree! I'm bringing the sock."
      },
      {
        "t": 410.65,
        "who": "Globo",
        "text": "Put me back in the drawer. Gently. Some of me is still in Antarctica."
      }
    ]
